{:db/id 17592186096697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186096497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-529" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186097300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89aee0c2-41c4-50d9-95e7-d07999f8e43b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 5x^{2} + Cx + D" :db/id 17592186095725} {:answer/id #uuid "a8264bab-88eb-5c1b-8cf6-4b5658e50c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Cx^{2} + 10x + D" :db/id 17592186095726} {:answer/id #uuid "c8066636-9a63-5ade-85aa-07f7e0be5cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + Cx + D" :db/id 17592186095727} {:answer/id #uuid "0a099fae-b25d-5abb-a50a-c5dc1eda7e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + D" :db/id 17592186095728} {:answer/id #uuid "1a40f9c2-33a9-5233-bec8-c51acd272979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{2} + Cx" :db/id 17592186095729}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8c860608-75a8-5451-a1a7-a0683cdbc84f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186095724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a4235d-41c9-5a0e-b658-3ec6b027584e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx" :db/id 17592186398891} {:answer/id #uuid "4b317621-35b4-53d1-939d-fab67d8f1031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398892} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf3415-556b-53c7-ac6f-21a5ddefd845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186398893} {:answer/id #uuid "a552a9bb-4de8-5448-90ec-372d3554e55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398894} {:answer/id #uuid "fa85c5fe-d852-5230-88fd-53d91e4bcd53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}+10x+D" :db/id 17592186398895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b317621-35b4-53d1-939d-fab67d8f1031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398892} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1789600-f409-5b68-8f83-26b29073f519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186398890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17751a3b-ed4b-5966-a450-330460c02715" :question/math-academy-id "q-50139" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186097301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d53-2adc-5935-a97d-5a586599fa37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id 17592186095731} {:answer/id #uuid "f4387418-5075-52c7-b685-924f2791a4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx^{2} + D" :db/id 17592186095732} {:answer/id #uuid "035eddbc-75fb-53ba-bc61-c5e0c4f3f025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id 17592186095733} {:answer/id #uuid "12887777-c8a6-5e16-bf30-ed290559c1e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{9}e^{3x} + Cx^{2} + D" :db/id 17592186095734} {:answer/id #uuid "7a68fbfe-8f1c-512a-8cc2-1b00fe18af49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + Cx + D" :db/id 17592186095735}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5a4615-20d2-563a-8738-3f3905aa1549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186095730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae67e9eb-7a6a-5f1f-9633-a869be11af9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186398897} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9d1539-56f7-56d2-9de6-1e9901343c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398898} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9ac46a-1dd6-5423-b497-fd6f6d213a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398899} {:answer/id #uuid "bd19773a-598c-5d4a-9935-ec0892a47ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398900} {:answer/id #uuid "89be7220-c09b-594d-8bff-c3a06f0b9221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186398901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9ac46a-1dd6-5423-b497-fd6f6d213a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398899} :answer-field/id #uuid "30eeac9f-d833-5bc8-934f-4097337092f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186398896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e04bc45-f34f-56fc-9a0e-8643597c4852" :question/math-academy-id "q-50148" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{3x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186253534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222d3811-c9d6-5e7d-b85c-95aa3edf1f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}+C{e}^{-x}+Dx" :db/id 17592186250846} {:answer/id #uuid "2aeffcd6-4797-59ec-860f-11aa268d3482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186250918} {:answer/id #uuid "65573541-1d72-5f2c-8753-db5f49766c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+C{x}^{2}+Dx" :db/id 17592186251383} {:answer/id #uuid "849fa2e5-06fb-57df-930c-0b02e73c0378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+C{e}^{-x}+D" :db/id 17592186251665} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1f009d-922c-586a-8478-6e8b8c14b8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aeffcd6-4797-59ec-860f-11aa268d3482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186250918} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdcccacb-a603-583d-bd6c-044e60d286f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31249dfd-dd0f-51ef-9508-c411dcb82eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119038" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=8{e}^{-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\int {e}^{-2x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x})+C \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{-2x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186253535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7a8ec6-3a7f-59a8-a36a-57b3c0e72d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186251298} {:answer/id #uuid "7c79b537-ac7c-5f96-ac1d-d1860fe46a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186251588} {:answer/id #uuid "bf97146c-f829-59ef-a419-71c2a32c9365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252244} {:answer/id #uuid "caaff75b-db1f-5d6d-9954-596535269be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252349} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a50774-5155-52c7-ab4f-ad427a04fce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186252719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a8ec6-3a7f-59a8-a36a-57b3c0e72d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186251298} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dba1b47-1418-53b5-8e0b-75806451ee28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186252943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39f64cb7-5a70-5fc4-8565-289e61a98a66" :question/math-academy-id "q-119041" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =4\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186253536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52771b76-d1b4-51e6-ab25-23d4a36b8b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=16{e}^{x/2}+{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186251227} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ba7691-e0f5-58b0-8306-ebfe9995ce2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252144} {:answer/id #uuid "d52fa157-a79b-5633-8056-0ad742ff72b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252440} {:answer/id #uuid "dad3e85e-1ff8-5404-b11a-42ecf755a4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=32{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252477} {:answer/id #uuid "e75f3c4e-0901-518a-8832-fdbd533170dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=32{e}^{x/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad3e85e-1ff8-5404-b11a-42ecf755a4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=32{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252477} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc11b9f-979a-5428-873c-d6f95fed2f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f306795-58e9-56b9-bfb8-50791b341767" :question/math-academy-id "q-119042" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6x=8{e}^{x/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=8{e}^{x/2}-6x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (8{e}^{x/2}-6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-6\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8(2{e}^{x/2})-6(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\  & =16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =16\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =16(2{e}^{x/2})-3(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})+Cx+D \\\\  & =32{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186253537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23cc8ac7-5e09-54ce-b6d3-e159d67ab8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186250861} {:answer/id #uuid "5901205a-e2f9-52f3-b94e-a09bbbbe0521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186251285} {:answer/id #uuid "60767b47-bf28-5927-9170-56b9e257c34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186251342} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1b67-45cb-51ac-a61f-ee18bbb149b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252358} {:answer/id #uuid "f4155827-e220-55c0-a8b5-d48c90df5a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-4{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1b67-45cb-51ac-a61f-ee18bbb149b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252358} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb1d879-87da-5436-bd2a-6290c0abb0e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5323a95-ccb4-5698-8e94-ec2b6a694be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-119043" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5=6{x}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-5x+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-5x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}-5x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186253538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061e9049-685a-5e57-b12d-6b56030be323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186250602} {:answer/id #uuid "42b59196-b9a8-573b-be83-9fd902357365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186251109} {:answer/id #uuid "b5960315-6cae-5802-922d-5b341c99ee9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252150} {:answer/id #uuid "c18c981b-dd26-58f1-bd40-912972cd4a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252261} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e95291-17f3-571b-b000-bb1e43ebce08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186252574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "061e9049-685a-5e57-b12d-6b56030be323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186250602} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27e7bd7-eaed-55b0-916c-a3dd3a5a5d99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186253193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f9dee74-c92e-5523-b157-7ba038c745c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119044" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{2}^{x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186332804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3120f347-6512-51ba-8814-9e94e5b4f3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186330382} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce95280-683d-5e12-8dc4-4bd3c4dab2ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186330490} {:answer/id #uuid "3f867192-f0f3-5837-8919-076f5a3bf835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186330514} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3c8989-f6d0-5647-ba9e-eebb2011d570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{4}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186332030} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd989d0-88b5-5bdf-841b-6beef220f5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186332184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ce95280-683d-5e12-8dc4-4bd3c4dab2ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186330490} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc205ab-aa57-5042-9e55-6ce79c5b03ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186332381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d572743-1005-5db1-a849-a7030811290a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50146" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $x\\cdot \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-4(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-2(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186348235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4239f824-0cdb-56c2-b03e-ba26979ede69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}\\,{x}^{3/2}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186345918} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0b8e6e-4ec7-58f9-81fa-5762778dd80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186346028} {:answer/id #uuid "517c9cdb-fafc-50c3-8c25-73b1c9f3a696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id 17592186346052} {:answer/id #uuid "8b73bf1b-fafc-5923-a918-fb2d34e27d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186346559} {:answer/id #uuid "995de1a6-1b5d-505e-b32d-431f2c275661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186346686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f0b8e6e-4ec7-58f9-81fa-5762778dd80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186346028} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14bd67c-bf99-5b34-a9b6-546acd076bc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49effcbe-cf5f-52f7-9899-b28e9803c299" :question/math-academy-id "q-79554" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\sqrt{x}=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{2}{5}\\,{x}^{5/2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186398200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2339eddd-c520-53fc-835c-406bf5642b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+C{x}^{2}+Dx" :db/id 17592186398202} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfc929f-0e67-5e47-80b6-d2949d1be4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{4}+C{x}^{2}+Dx" :db/id 17592186398203} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd5ffc9-d74f-592b-a383-53ba3803f136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398204} {:answer/id #uuid "395e4984-cf1d-534b-b01f-09b03aa00ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{5}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398205} {:answer/id #uuid "796c5808-234b-5385-a5e5-513d3b55563c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{4}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd5ffc9-d74f-592b-a383-53ba3803f136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186398204} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7d8f65-a93b-5daa-b8fa-34a0b4f4a5ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186398201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f503fbb-9abb-5178-8aa2-5b5187c150ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-119039" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=20{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =20\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5{x}^{4}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+Cx+D \\\\  & ={x}^{5}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id 17592186399336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186399338} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc1b791-33fe-5cbe-8db2-7cec317b3415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id 17592186399339} {:answer/id #uuid "9947a22d-6748-5635-9bff-c054c9d5412e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186399340} {:answer/id #uuid "a32a5598-4875-50b4-a207-219749ce447a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C+D" :db/id 17592186399341} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea83b4-6e8e-5133-9645-9dd52053a38b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186399342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "badd1b57-14e7-5b56-b3e2-4f76bf483962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id 17592186399338} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d542a7-e1e6-5040-8811-6896030adc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99338d6e-762d-5e46-924e-c9b8ff055bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99009" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}
